        "type": "heading",
        "snippet": "Which Should You Choose?"
      },
      {
        "type": "list",
        "list": [
          {
            "snippet": "Guidelines: Major medical guidelines (like the American College of Cardiology and American Heart Association) give both classes an equal Class I first-line recommendation ."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "Historical vs. Modern Preference: Traditionally, doctors often started with an ACE inhibitor because of older, extensive trial data. However, modern real-world data and safety reviews show ARBs work just as well while causing significantly fewer side effects like cough and angioedema."
          },
          {
            "snippet": "When to avoid both: Neither medication should be used during pregnancy, and both can raise blood potassium levels (hyperkalemia) or affect kidney function, requiring basic lab monitoring. You should never take an ACE inhibitor and an ARB together ."
          }
        ]
